halui.auto-inhibit and halui.mdi-inhibit refuse before anything starts.

Without them the only way to forbid automatic motion is to let AUTO start and
then drop the machine's enable, which reaches the operator as an E-stop they
did not cause and which says nothing about why.  These refuse the mode change,
the program run and the MDI command outright, with a message naming the
inhibit.

Two pins rather than one, because the granularity matters in practice: an
interlock that forbids running a program usually still has to allow the MDI
routines an operator homes and touches off with, and [HALUI]MDI_COMMAND
entries reach the machine through the same MDI path.  The test pins down that
each inhibit leaves the other alone, which is the property a single combined
pin would quietly lose.

mdi-inhibit deliberately covers [HALUI]MDI_COMMAND as well: an inhibit that
some MDI sources bypass would be worse than none.
